# Documentation purpose and style

Reference documents describe implemented behavior, inputs, outputs, equations,
assumptions, interfaces, failure modes and verified boundaries. Explain a design
choice through its scientific or engineering justification. Keep development
chronology, issue IDs and resolved-bug narratives in project records.

Write direct, connected prose. Define technical terms and every equation symbol
at first use; specify units, index domains, coordinates and time levels. Give a
worked example when it clarifies an algorithm. Avoid formulaic “A, not B” framing.
An overview serves a scientifically trained newcomer; a technical reference must
let a developer trace an input through source/artifacts to a tested output.

Use display LaTeX for governing equations and readable inline mathematics for
short expressions. Inspect equations in the intended Markdown/PDF renderer.
Describe time stepping, interpolation, solvers and parameterization separately.
Distinguish measured results, hypotheses and planned capabilities. Bound every
validation claim by its inputs, oracle, tolerances, configuration and environment.
Document code and scientific assumptions in the same patch as their implementation.
